    "markdown": "**Top of mind:** Google DeepMind is now run day-to-day by Koray Kavukcuoglu after Demis Hassabis moved up to chairman, and Jeff Dean left Google after 27 years to co-found Discovery Loop — a reshuffle that puts vendor relationships at one of the biggest human-data buyers in play and creates a brand-new science-focused lab to sell into. Meanwhile, Anthropic disclosed a 20-year, $9.1B compute deal with Riot Platforms as part of roughly $71B in compute commitments — a strong signal that frontier training demand, and the data spend that follows it, keeps compounding.\n\n## Competitor moves\n\n- **Fallout continues from Forbes' exposé on US data vendors selling to Chinese labs.** Forbes (Aug 5) reported Mercor booked ~2% of Q2 revenue from Chinese labs on a >$2B annualized run rate, Surge AI CEO Edwin Chen traveled to China to court lab executives, and Turing project documents point to ByteDance work; Micro1 CEO Ali Ansari publicly countered that his firm won't sell data to adversarial nations. Expect labs and Washington to tighten vendor-compliance requirements — a clean geo-compliance posture is becoming a sales weapon. [Forbes](https://www.forbes.com/sites/annatong/2026/08/05/silicon-valleys-other-china-problem-its-training-their-ai/), [TechBuzz](https://www.techbuzz.ai/articles/meet-the-billionaires-selling-ai-its-training-data)\n- **Mercor's H1 2026 revenue reportedly passed $615M (~90% from frontier labs); valuation talk at ~$20B, with Surge AI pegged at $25B+.** The Information reports revenue surging at both Mercor and Handshake on demand for human contractors to train AI. The market keeps growing fast, but extreme customer concentration means pricing power sits with a handful of lab buyers — diversification beyond frontier labs is the open flank. [The Information](https://www.theinformation.com/articles/handshake-mercor-revenue-surges-demand-human-contractors-train-ai), [BigGo Finance](https://finance.biggo.com/news/865c23cb-3e0d-4c21-aa0d-6bf2a7fc8d1a), [TechBuzz](https://www.techbuzz.ai/articles/meet-the-billionaires-selling-ai-its-training-data)\n\n## New projects & opportunities\n\n- **River AI raised $1.1B (Aug 11) to help enterprises train, tune, and serve their own models.** xAI co-founder Igor Babuschkin's startup is backed by General Catalyst, AMP PBC, NVIDIA, and AMD Ventures; a wave of enterprise custom-model building means every deployment needs SFT, RLHF, and eval data — a direct services opening below the frontier-lab tier. [AIwire](https://www.hpcwire.com/aiwire/2026/08/11/river-ai-secures-1-1b-to-make-custom-ai-models-easier-to-train-and-deploy/)\n- **OpenAI launched GPT-5.6-Cyber for vetted security defenders (Aug 11).** Specialized frontier models in high-stakes domains need scarce domain-expert data and red-team evals; cybersecurity expert sourcing — where India has a deep talent pool — is an underserved, high-rate niche. [Tech Startups](https://techstartups.com/2026/08/11/top-tech-news-today-august-11-2026-anthropic-intel-meta-openai-nvidia-unitree-more/)\n- **India angle: Activate AI, a $75M India-focused AI fund (backer of unicorn Sarvam), profiled Aug 11.** More funded Indian model startups means more local demand for Indic-language, domain-specific data and evals — customers who prefer India-based delivery. [Tech Startups](https://techstartups.com/2026/08/11/top-tech-news-today-august-11-2026-anthropic-intel-meta-openai-nvidia-unitree-more/) (orig. Rest of World)\n- **Alibaba is releasing Qwen3.8 open weights (reported Aug 10).** Frontier-scale open weights accelerate enterprise fine-tuning, shifting data demand from pre-training scale toward high-quality post-training sets that mid-market clients must buy rather than build. [BuildFastWithAI](https://www.buildfastwithai.com/blogs/ai-news-today-august-10-2026)\n\n## Frontier lab movements\n\n- **Anthropic signed a 20-year, ~$9.1B compute agreement with Riot Platforms (~191MW at Rockdale, TX), part of ~$71B in total compute commitments.** Multi-decade compute buildouts imply years of continued frontier training runs — the core driver of human-data and eval spend. [Tech Startups](https://techstartups.com/2026/08/11/top-tech-news-today-august-11-2026-anthropic-intel-meta-openai-nvidia-unitree-more/)\n- **Meta announced a pivot toward open-source AI (Aug 11).** If Meta's frontier work goes open, model differentiation shifts further toward proprietary data and post-training quality — structurally good for data vendors, and worth watching for what it means for Meta-owned Scale AI's lab business. [WBUR](https://www.wbur.org/hereandnow/2026/08/11/meta-open-source-ai)\n- **OpenAI is reportedly targeting an IPO as soon as September 2026.** Public-market capital would fund even bigger data programs, but quarterly scrutiny could push labs toward outcome-based pricing and consolidation onto fewer, audited vendors. [BuildFastWithAI](https://www.buildfastwithai.com/blogs/ai-news-today-august-10-2026)\n\n## Research papers\n\n- **SWE-Bench ProMax: an expert-curated, multilingual code-refactoring benchmark (Yuling Shi et al., Aug 10).** 170 instances across seven languages, with issue descriptions rewritten from scratch and test suites manually reviewed; the best frontier model resolves only 41.2%. Takeaway: labs still pay for painstaking human curation, and agentic-coding evals are far from saturated — a concrete, sellable SKU. [arXiv](https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.09802), [Hugging Face](https://huggingface.co/papers/2608.09802)\n- **Contamination-proof evaluation via adversarial fast-moving real-world domains (William Bolton et al., ICML 2026 AI-for-Science workshop).** Uses 2026 F1 car-design innovations and freshly rotated Magic: The Gathering card pools as post-cutoff ground truth models can't have memorized. The methodology is a template for continuously refreshed eval subscriptions — evals that expire by design create recurring revenue. [arXiv](https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03569v1)\n\n## Leadership movements\n\n- **Demis Hassabis stepped back from running Google DeepMind; Koray Kavukcuoglu, a 13-year DeepMind veteran, takes day-to-day control as SVP.** Hassabis becomes chairman and Alphabet chief scientist. New operational leadership at one of the largest data buyers means vendor relationships and procurement priorities are in play — worth refreshing DeepMind contacts now. [Axios](https://www.axios.com/2026/08/05/google-deepmind-demis-hassabis-ai), [The Decoder](https://the-decoder.com/google-deepmind-loses-both-its-ceo-and-chief-scientist-as-demis-hassabis-and-jeff-dean-step-down-simultaneously/)\n- **Jeff Dean left Google after 27 years to co-found Discovery Loop with Sanjay Ghemawat; Oriol Vinyals and Quoc Le are joining.** The Google-backed public benefit corporation aims to speed up ML-driven discoveries in science and engineering — a brand-new prospective buyer of expert scientific data and evaluation, worth an early BD approach. [The Decoder](https://the-decoder.com/google-deepmind-loses-both-its-ceo-and-chief-scientist-as-demis-hassabis-and-jeff-dean-step-down-simultaneously/), [TNW](https://thenextweb.com/news/google-deepmind-shakeup-hassabis-jeff-dean-vinyals-discovery-loop)\n- **Chloé Bakalar, OpenAI's head of ethics, departed less than a year after joining (Aug 11, via FT).** Another senior governance exit at OpenAI amid safety scrutiny — relevant to how labs structure oversight of data-sourcing and human-feedback pipelines, and the compliance bar vendors will be held to. [Tech Startups](https://techstartups.com/2026/08/11/top-tech-news-today-august-11-2026-anthropic-intel-meta-openai-nvidia-unitree-more/)"
