    "markdown": "**Top of mind:** Forbes' investigation into US data vendors quietly selling human-preference data to Chinese labs (Surge AI, Mercor, Turing named) is snowballing into a Washington policy fight over export controls on training data — a potential contract reshuffle that favors vendors with clean China exposure. Meanwhile Google DeepMind's leadership shakeup (Hassabis to chairman, Jeff Dean out after 27 years to found Discovery Loop) signals an even more aggressive frontier race, which means sustained demand for expert data and evals.\n\n## Competitor moves\n\n- **Forbes exposé: Surge AI, Mercor and Turing tied to a ~$500M/yr US-to-China training-data trade.** Tencent, Ant, Alibaba and ByteDance reportedly buy the same human-preference datasets supplied to OpenAI and Anthropic — while Surge and Mercor also hold US government contracts, inviting FOCI/export-control scrutiny. If Washington acts, security-sensitive contracts could migrate to vendors with no China exposure. [Forbes](https://www.forbes.com/sites/annatong/2026/08/05/silicon-valleys-other-china-problem-its-training-their-ai/), [ChinaTalk](https://www.chinatalk.media/p/the-mercor-hack-and-the-commoditization), [AI Weekly](https://aiweekly.co/alerts/surge-ai-mercor-reportedly-sell-training-data-to-chinese-labs)\n- **Mercor's breach fallout keeps compounding — at least seven federal lawsuits now filed.** The March supply-chain hack (~4TB including contractor IDs, biometrics and interview videos) has become the industry's cautionary tale on contractor-data security; expect customers to tighten vendor security audits, which is a differentiator for whoever can pass them. [Staffing Industry Analysts](https://www.staffingindustry.com/news/global-daily-news/ai-staffing-firm-mercor-faces-lawsuits-over-data-breach), [PYMNTS](https://www.pymnts.com/legal/2026/ai-startup-mercor-faces-lawsuit-over-data-breach/), [ChinaTalk](https://www.chinatalk.media/p/the-mercor-hack-and-the-commoditization)\n\n## New projects & opportunities\n\n- **EU AI Act Article 10 fully enforced since Aug 2 — annotation and labeling are now regulated data-governance operations for high-risk AI.** Compliance-grade labeling (documented provenance, QA trails, human oversight per Article 14) is becoming a paid requirement, not a nice-to-have — a concrete pitch for vendors who can offer auditable pipelines to EU-exposed customers. [Kili Technology](https://kili-technology.com/blog/best-on-premise-data-labeling-platforms-for-regulated-industries-2026-guide), [Humans in the Loop](https://humansintheloop.org/data-annotation-trends-2026-forecast-best-practices/)\n- **China-exposure scrutiny is creating a \"neutral vendor\" opening.** With US labs and federal buyers now asking who else their data suppliers serve, vendors that can attest to clean customer lists have a new sales wedge for defense- and government-adjacent RLHF/eval work. [Forbes](https://www.forbes.com/sites/annatong/2026/08/05/silicon-valleys-other-china-problem-its-training-their-ai/)\n\n## Frontier lab movements\n\n- **Anthropic disclosed ~$71B in compute commitments and launched the \"Theseus\" datacenter venture with Macquarie and GIC.** Compute build-outs at this scale telegraph bigger training runs ahead — historically a leading indicator of post-training data and eval spend. [Michael Parekh / RTZ](https://michaelparekh.substack.com/p/ai-weekly-summary-rtz-1004)\n- **Meta released Muse Glimmer (Aug 10): a 30B open-weights multimodal model tuned for agentic tool use and LLM-as-judge.** A capable free judge model lowers the cost floor for automated eval — pushing human-data vendors further up-market toward expert adjudication and hard cases. [Price Per Token](https://pricepertoken.com/news/model-releases), [AI Weekly](https://aiweekly.co/ai-news-today)\n- **OpenAI's GPT-5.6-Cyber is its first model rated 'High' for cyber capability under the Preparedness Framework.** Crossing capability thresholds forces more red-teaming and safety evaluation — a growing demand line for specialized cyber eval and adversarial data. [Price Per Token](https://pricepertoken.com/news/model-releases)\n\n## Research papers\n\n- **CoT-Core: CoT-aware coreset selection to slash LLM evaluation cost** — Qihua Pan, Zhenheng Tang, Peijie Dong, Xiang Liu et al. Picking small, representative eval subsets that preserve ranking fidelity could change how labs buy eval data: fewer, higher-value items. [arXiv](https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00014)\n- **Agentic Coding in the Wild: GitHub Copilot traces at production scale** — Banruo Liu, Haoran Qiu, Íñigo Goiri, Rodrigo Fonseca, Ricardo Bianchini, Esha Choukse (Microsoft). Production agent-trace datasets show where coding agents fail — a map of exactly the failure modes labs will pay human experts to annotate and repair. [arXiv](https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00101)\n- **Can LLM Agents Price Competitively? A dynamic multi-attribute auction benchmark for agentic commerce** — Shimaa Ahmed, Yiwei Cai, Mohsen Minaei, Rahul Rachuri. New agentic-commerce benchmarks mean new eval-harness and scenario-authoring work as labs chase agent reliability in transactions. [arXiv](https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00102)\n\n## Leadership movements\n\n- **Demis Hassabis stepped back from running Google DeepMind (Aug 5); Koray Kavukcuoglu takes day-to-day control and the Gemini roadmap.** Hassabis becomes DeepMind chairman and Alphabet chief scientist; a Kavukcuoglu-led, ship-faster DeepMind likely means more post-training and eval cycles. [Axios](https://www.axios.com/2026/08/05/google-deepmind-demis-hassabis-ai), [The Decoder](https://the-decoder.com/google-deepmind-loses-both-its-ceo-and-chief-scientist-as-demis-hassabis-and-jeff-dean-step-down-simultaneously/)\n- **Jeff Dean left Google after 27 years to co-found Discovery Loop with Sanjay Ghemawat, Oriol Vinyals and Quoc Le; Google is a founding investor.** A new well-funded lab focused on AI-for-science is a brand-new prospective buyer of expert scientific data — worth tracking their first data partnerships. Alphabet shed roughly $160-200B in market value on the news. [Quartz](https://qz.com/jeff-dean-google-chief-scientist-discovery-loop-startup-080526), [HNGN](https://www.hngn.com/articles/272649/20260808/google-chief-scientist-jeff-dean-departs-after-27-years-launch-new-discovery-loop-startup.htm)"
